# kk: Emit 32-bit fdiv as fast::divide
# https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/mesa/mesa/-/merge_requests/46404 state=opened
# ### What does this MR do and why?

<!--Describe in detail what your merge request does and why.-->

```
kk: Emit 32-bit fdiv as fast::divide

Shaders whose float controls require no contraction or reassociation
get their ALU ops inside a "#pragma METAL fp math_mode(safe)" block.
There, '/' is correctly rounded, and in shaders that divide a lot it is
most of the cost of the safe block.  fast::divide is not affected by
the pragma.

fast::divide is not correctly rounded: the largest error measured on an
Apple M5 Pro (macOS 27.2) is 1.7 ULP, against the 2.5 ULP Vulkan allows
for 32-bit floating-point division.  Infinities, NaNs and signed zeros
are not changed by this.

Integer division keeps '/'.  fast::divide only takes floating-point
operands, and an integer quotient computed through it is off by one
for values as small as 33 / 33.

Assisted-by: LLM
```
--- src/kosmickrisp/compiler/nir_to_msl.c
+++ src/kosmickrisp/compiler/nir_to_msl.c
@@ -317,9 +317,19 @@ alu_to_msl(struct nir_to_msl_ctx *ctx, nir_alu_instr *instr)
       break;
    case nir_op_idiv:
    case nir_op_udiv:
-   case nir_op_fdiv:
       ALU_BINOP("/");
       break;
+   case nir_op_fdiv:
+      /* '/' inside a math_mode(safe) block is correctly rounded and much
+       * slower.  fast::divide ignores the pragma; it is not correctly rounded
+       * (up to 1.7 ULP measured on Apple M5), which is within the 2.5 ULP
+       * Vulkan allows for 32-bit division.  It only exists for float types.
+       */
+      if (instr->def.bit_size == 32)
+         alu_funclike(ctx, instr, "fast::divide");
+      else
+         ALU_BINOP("/");
+      break;
    case nir_op_irem:
       ALU_BINOP("%");
       break;

